For cross-subscription VNet peering, we use both `vnet_peerings` and `vnet_peerings_v1` depending on which subscription owns the source VNet.

The normal `vnet_peerings` implementation uses the AzureRM provider, so it creates the peering in the provider’s configured subscription. This is fine when the source VNet is in the pipeline/default subscription.

For the reverse peering, where the source VNet is in another subscription, `vnet_peerings_v1` is used. It uses AzAPI and the source VNet’s full Azure resource ID as the `parent_id`, so Azure knows exactly which subscription contains the VNet.

Example:

```hcl
parent_id = "/subscriptions/<subscription-id>/resourceGroups/<rg>/providers/Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/<vnet-name>"
```

Because the subscription is part of the resource ID, we do not need to switch the pipeline’s default subscription just to create the remote-side peering.

The usual pattern is:

```text
Local/default subscription → Remote subscription
    use vnet_peerings

Remote subscription → Local/default subscription
    use vnet_peerings_v1
```

A typical tfvars configuration looks like this:

```hcl
# Local → Remote
vnet_peerings = {
  spoke_to_hub = {
    name = "spoke-to-hub"

    from = {
      vnet_key = "spoke_vnet"
    }

    to = {
      lz_key   = "networking_hub"
      vnet_key = "hub_vnet"
    }

    allow_virtual_network_access = true
    allow_forwarded_traffic      = true
    allow_gateway_transit        = false
    use_remote_gateways          = true
  }
}

# Remote → Local
vnet_peerings_v1 = {
  hub_to_spoke = {
    name = "hub-to-spoke"

    from = {
      lz_key   = "networking_hub"
      vnet_key = "hub_vnet"
    }

    to = {
      vnet_key = "spoke_vnet"
    }

    allow_virtual_network_access = true
    allow_forwarded_traffic      = true
    allow_gateway_transit        = true
    use_remote_gateways          = false
  }
}
```

When checking whether a landing zone supports `vnet_peerings_v1`, there are a few places to look.

First, check the root variable definition. There should be something like:

```hcl
variable "vnet_peerings_v1" {
  type    = any
  default = {}
}
```

Then check the root `module.tf` and make sure the variable is actually passed into the child landing-zone module:

```hcl
networking = {
  vnets             = var.vnets
  vnet_peerings     = var.vnet_peerings
  vnet_peerings_v1  = var.vnet_peerings_v1
  vhub_peerings     = var.vhub_peerings
}
```

If `vnet_peerings_v1` exists in the child module but is missing here, values from the tfvars will never reach the implementation.

Next, check the child module, for example:

```text
./aztf/no-vm-prefix
```

Look at the `networking` variable definition and confirm it accepts `vnet_peerings_v1`. Depending on how the module is written, it may look similar to:

```hcl
variable "networking" {
  type = any
}
```

or, if it uses a typed object:

```hcl
variable "networking" {
  type = object({
    vnets            = any
    vnet_peerings    = any
    vnet_peerings_v1 = any
    vhub_peerings    = any
  })
}
```

Then check any locals that rebuild the networking object. For example:

```hcl
locals {
  networking = {
    vnets            = var.networking.vnets
    vnet_peerings    = var.networking.vnet_peerings
    vnet_peerings_v1 = var.networking.vnet_peerings_v1
  }
}
```

This is important because the value can be declared and passed into the module but still get dropped if a local object does not copy it through.

Finally, search the child module for the actual resource implementation:

```text
vnet_peerings_v1
```

You should find an AzAPI resource or module logic similar to:

```hcl
resource "azapi_resource" "vnet_peerering_v1" {
  for_each = local.networking.vnet_peerings_v1

  type      = "Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/virtualNetworkPeerings@<api-version>"
  name      = each.value.name
  parent_id = <resolved source vnet full resource id>

  body = {
    properties = {
      remoteVirtualNetwork = {
        id = <resolved destination vnet id>
      }

      allowVirtualNetworkAccess = each.value.allow_virtual_network_access
      allowForwardedTraffic     = each.value.allow_forwarded_traffic
      allowGatewayTransit       = each.value.allow_gateway_transit
      useRemoteGateways         = each.value.use_remote_gateways
    }
  }
}
```

The most important part to confirm is that `parent_id` resolves to the source VNet’s full ID, including the subscription:

```hcl
/subscriptions/<subscription-id>/resourceGroups/<rg>/providers/Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/<vnet>
```

So the full flow to verify is:

```text
peering.tfvars
    ↓
var.vnet_peerings_v1
    ↓
root module networking.vnet_peerings_v1
    ↓
child module var.networking.vnet_peerings_v1
    ↓
local.networking.vnet_peerings_v1
    ↓
AzAPI resource
    ↓
source VNet full resource ID used as parent_id
```

If all of those are present, the landing zone is wired correctly for cross-subscription peering.

The pipeline identity still needs RBAC permissions on both VNets. `vnet_peerings_v1` changes how the resource is addressed, but it does not bypass Azure permissions.
